IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cbr-nouns`

-- Child Benefit (Rates) Regulations 2006 -- LAYER 1, MODULE 2: REGULATION 1
-- (citation, commencement and interpretation), plus the date of making in the
-- title block (src:4).
--
-- Dates are written day first, `Date day month year`: `YMD` is not in the stable
-- prelude (NOTES.md, "What the stable binary does not accept").
--
-- Not encoded here, and why (NOTES.md has the whole table):
--   * the citation half of reg 1(1), and the abbreviations "SSCBA" and "SSCB(NI)A"
--     in reg 1(2): pure citations, `inert`;
--   * "immediately after the Child Benefit (General) Regulations 2006": that
--     instrument is not among the inputs, and two instruments in force on one day
--     cannot be ordered within the day by a date (fork F9).

§ `Regulation 1: citation, commencement and interpretation`

§§ `The dates: made on 29 March 2006, in force from 10 April 2006`

-- src:4 | Made 29th March 2006
@ref title block, "Made 29th March 2006" (src:4)
`the date these Regulations were made` MEANS Date 29 3 2006

-- src:9 | (1) These Regulations may be cited as the Child Benefit (Rates) Regulations 2006 and shall come into force on 10th April 2006 immediately after the Child Benefit (General) Regulations 2006 .
@ref reg 1(1), commencement (src:9)
`the date these Regulations come into force` MEANS Date 10 4 2006

-- The text speaks of "any week" and never defines one ("week" is the parent Act's
-- word, out of text). Fork F7: a week is tested at its first day, as reg 1(3)
-- tests every fact, so these Regulations are in force in a week if they are in
-- force on its first day. A week that began before 10 April 2006 is therefore
-- outside them.
@ref reg 1(1), commencement (src:9)
GIVEN `first day of the week` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`these Regulations are in force in the week beginning` `first day of the week` MEANS
    `first day of the week` AT LEAST `the date these Regulations come into force`

-- NOT A PROVISION OF THE TEXT. The text states £27.05 and £17.90 (reg 2(1)) and dates
-- neither figure. The version of the text that was encoded is dated 2026-04-06
-- (../../registers/source-bundle/uksi-2006-965.meta.json: "version: 2026-04-06", the point in time of the latest revised
-- version), so its figures are known for a week that begins on or after that date and
-- for no earlier week (fork F8). Delete this one definition, and the rule that reads
-- it in cbr-reg2-rate, to answer for every week from commencement.
-- out of text: ../../registers/source-bundle/uksi-2006-965.meta.json
@ref ../../registers/source-bundle/uksi-2006-965.meta.json, version 2026-04-06 (not part of the text)
`the date of the version of the text encoded` MEANS Date 6 4 2026

§§ `Regulation 1(2): "qualifying young person"`

-- "child" is not defined in these Regulations (it is the parent Act's word), so
-- the text gives no rule for it; it is asked as a fact in cbr-nouns.
-- Whether a person "is such a person for the purposes of Part 9" of the Act is
-- out of text (SSCBA Part 9, SSCB(NI)A Part 9), asked as a fact.
-- src:13 | “qualifying young person” means a person—
-- src:14 | (a) in Great Britain, who is such a person for the purposes of Part 9 of SSCBA; and
-- src:15 | (b) in Northern Ireland, who is such a person for the purposes of Part 9 of SSCB(NI)A.
@ref reg 1(2) "qualifying young person" (src:13-15)
GIVEN `where the young person is` IS A `Where a young person is`
      `a qualifying young person for the purposes of Part 9 of SSCBA` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a qualifying young person for the purposes of Part 9 of SSCB(NI)A` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a person is a qualifying young person for the purposes of these Regulations` `where the young person is` `a qualifying young person for the purposes of Part 9 of SSCBA` `a qualifying young person for the purposes of Part 9 of SSCB(NI)A` MEANS
        (   (`where the young person is` EQUALS `in Great Britain`)
        AND `a qualifying young person for the purposes of Part 9 of SSCBA`)           -- (a)
    OR  (   (`where the young person is` EQUALS `in Northern Ireland`)
        AND `a qualifying young person for the purposes of Part 9 of SSCB(NI)A`)       -- (b)

§§ `Regulation 1(3): facts are tested at the beginning of the week`

-- The day is the first day of the week. Every `@desc` in the seat layer says "at the
-- start of the week" because of this rule.
-- src:16 | (3) References in these Regulations to any condition being satisfied or any facts existing shall be construed as references to the condition being satisfied or the facts existing at the beginning of that week.
@ref reg 1(3) (src:16)
GIVEN `first day of the week` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day on which conditions and facts are tested in the week beginning` `first day of the week` MEANS
    `first day of the week`
